  "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{\u2032}$ are \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
